Subject: vkd3d-shader: Emit "rep i#" for SM2/SM3 loops bounded by an int uniform.

For "for (i = 0; i < n; ++i)" with an int uniform n, native compilers put n in
an integer constant register and emit "rep i#", so the application sets the
trip count. vkd3d already identifies n as the loop limiter, but then aborts
with "Loops with user-defined limiter int uniform". TrackMania car skins are
vs_2_0 and bound both light loops this way, so the vertex shader fails and the
body is drawn with the material color and no skin.

Give the limiter its own i# register, report it in the constant table as
D3DXRS_INT4 with an integer default ({n, 0, 1, 0}, as native writes it), emit
"rep" on that register and drop the per-iteration condition checks, which the
counter now enforces. Limiters "rep" cannot take (a component other than x, or
a non-integer type) are still reported as not implemented.

Based on Wine 9f8c4ae4d35. Run ./src/shell/tools/probes/smoke-shaders.sh.

